    <item>
      <title>A supergene and genomic mosaicism are associated with phenotypic evolution in Galápagos tree finches</title>
      <link>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-78507-8</link>
      <content:encoded>
                &lt;p&gt;Nature Communications, Published online: 11 October 2026; &lt;a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-78507-8"&gt;doi:10.1038/s41467-026-78507-8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;Adaptive radiations produce striking phenotypic diversity over remarkably short evolutionary timescales. This study uses genomic analyses of Darwin’s tree finches to reveal a cryptic lineage, in which autosomal differentiation with another sympatric species is restricted primarily to a single locus (G03) that approaches fixation, shifting them from one phenotypic optimum to another.</content:encoded>
      <dc:title>A supergene and genomic mosaicism are associated with phenotypic evolution in Galápagos tree finches</dc:title>
